--- @param age number

--- @return <type>
--- @return MyClassObject

--- @note <text>
--- @note when implementing this use XXX and not YYY

--- @include <path_to_file>
--- @include folder/Class.java
(this flag is used to give context to the llm eg when passed like above the llm will get appeneded to the message "Additional content:\n Class.java\n <filecontent". This will be used to guide the llm to a class implementation or something similiar for more accurate code generation)

--- @
(empty tag!)


2. We will use treesitter/LSP for making the marks look coloured (like they are part of the program). 

3. when our cursor is between a set of marks or on top of such and we press <leader>G we ask the llm to generate code inside of the current block of marks. Example we have this code:
--- @param age number
--- @return bool
def is_of_age(age):

--- @ 
If our cursor is on any of those for lines and we press <leader>G an animation should start in the block. When we get the reposnce it should be something like this:
--- @param age number
--- @return bool
def is_of_age(age):
    if(age>18):
	    return True
	else:
	    return False
--- @ 

(important thing is that we keep params between generation!)

In this the LLM saw something like:
system: You are a coding assistant. @param A B tag means the next block of code uses param_name A that is of type B. @return ...... You should output the hole code that sits between the metatags. Your responce will overwrite anything in between so if you want to keep something write it out again.
user: Suggest a new code between the block of params:
--- @param age number
--- @return bool
def is_of_age(age):

--- @ 

Then the llm suggests something and whatever it suggests gets replaced into the file.

Thought of these generation modes:
<leader>G - the llm gets context only form the param blocks
<leader>gb - the llm gets context from the hole buffer
<leader>gf - the llm gets context from the hole buffer and a list of all non-gitignored files and foldersr next to where the buffer lives.
<leader>gc - same as above but instead of the pwd of the buffer the pwd of the neovim process. This is because when I code I start neovim from the project root. (gc is short for generate from codebase)

At any point the LLM gets a "tools" list of tools where he has "read_file" and can be called with a list of files. This is for the llm to gather more context - it can see that not enought info is presant and can see that some file names indicate their are relevant so it uses read_files("file_a","file_b"...). At some point when the final info is gathered it will respond and snap code in place. 
Another tool is just "list_dir" with a path but this should not show gitignored or dotfiles. 

